.TH RATCH 1 "2026-10-15" "ratch 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ratch \- detachable terminal sessions without terminal emulation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ratch
.RI [ options ]
.I session
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B ratch
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ratch
keeps a terminal program running after you detach from it and lets you attach
again later, from the same terminal or another. It does not emulate a
terminal: bytes from the program reach your terminal unchanged, and your keys
reach the program unchanged.
.PP
With only a session name,
.B ratch
attaches to that session, first creating it if it is not running. Without a
command a session runs the user's shell.
.PP
A session name containing a slash is taken as the path of the session's
socket.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI attach " session"
Attach; fail if the session is not running.
.TP
.BI new " session " [ command ...]
Create a session and attach.
.TP
.BI start " session " [ command ...]
Create a session without attaching.
.TP
.BI run " session " [ command ...]
Create a session and remain in the foreground as its master.
.TP
.BI push " session"
Send standard input to the session as if typed.
.TP
.BR kill " [" \-f ] " \fIsession"
Stop a session: SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after five seconds.
.B \-f
sends SIGKILL at once.
.TP
.BR wait " [" \-t " \fIseconds\fR] \fIsession"
Wait for a session to end and exit with the program's status: its exit code,
128 plus the signal number if a signal killed it, 124 if the time ran out,
255 if the session ended without recording a status.
.TP
.BR log " [" \-f "] [" \-n " \fIN\fR] \fIsession"
Print the session's output, or its last
.I N
lines.
.B \-f
keeps following a running session.
.TP
.BR tail " [" \-f "] [" \-n " \fIN\fR] \fIsession"
The same, the last 10 lines by default.
.TP
.BR clear " [\fIsession\fR]"
Forget the session's history. Without a name, the session the command is run
in.
.TP
.BR list " [" \-a "] [" \-\-names ]
List sessions.
.B \-a
includes finished ones;
.B \-\-names
prints names only.
.TP
.BI rm " session"
.TQ
.B rm \-a
Remove the log and other files of a finished or dead session, or of all of
them.
.TP
.BI rename " session new-name"
Rename a running session.
.TP
.BR env " [" \-\-sh | \-\-fish "] [\fIsession\fR]"
Print shell commands that set the terminal-related environment variables to
those of the terminal that attached last. Use as
.BR "eval \(dq$(ratch env)\(dq" .
.TP
.B current
Print the session this shell is running in; with nested sessions, the chain.
Exits 1 outside a session.
.SH OPTIONS
Options go before the command or after the session name.
.TP
.BI \-e " char"
Detach character, given as a character or in caret notation. Default
.BR ^\e .
.TP
.B \-E
No detach character.
.TP
.BI \-n " lines"
History to show on attach. Default: the last 128 kB. 0 shows none.
.TP
.B \-\-ro
Attach read-only.
.TP
.BR \-R " " none | clear
.B clear
wipes the screen and its scrollback before attaching.
.TP
.BR \-r " " none | ctrl_l | winch
How to ask the program to redraw after attaching. Default
.BR winch :
the window size is set, and when it is the same as before, it is made one
column narrower for a moment first, since many programs repaint only for a
size that really changed.
While the program repaints, a terminal that knows synchronized output holds
its picture still, then shows the result at once.
.TP
.B \-H
Keep the session open with a shell after the command ends.
.TP
.BI \-C " size"
Log size limit, with an optional k or m suffix. 0 keeps no log. Default 1m.
.TP
.B \-z
Do not interpret the suspend key.
.TP
.B \-q
Suppress messages.
.TP
.B \-t
Send the terminal no escape sequences.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RATCH_OPTS
Options applied before those on the command line.
.TP
.B RATCH_SESSION_DIR
Directory to keep sessions in.
.TP
.B RATCH_ENV_VARS
Further variable names for
.B env
to carry, separated by spaces or commas.
.TP
.B RATCH_SESSION
Set inside a session to its socket path; for nested sessions, the paths
separated by colons, outermost first.
.TP
.B SHELL
The program a session runs when no command is given.
.PP
The names follow the name the program is run under: a copy called
.B foo
uses
.BR FOO_OPTS ,
.B FOO_SESSION
and so on.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/ratch/
Sessions. Must belong to the user and have mode 0700. Each session has a
socket named after it and, beside it,
.I .log
(output),
.I .info
(process ids, command, how it ended) and
.I .env
(environment of the terminal that attached last).
.TP
.I /tmp/.ratch\-UID/
Used when there is no home directory.
.SH SECURITY
Any process running as the user can attach to, type into and stop the user's
sessions. Nothing but the socket's permissions keeps other users out.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dtach (1),
.BR screen (1),
.BR tmux (1)
